  "worked_solution": "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(0)=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/576d25a420f1f4ebcbd0e9b055d7a27159caa8f1721ff5f35a32b86c3e3a0fb3.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is only one stationary point, and its $x$-coordinate is $x=0.$\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{\u2032}(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
